<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Uryankhay Krai</h1><p class="st-meta">1914 CE – 1921 CE · capital Belotsarsk (Kyzyl)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Russian protectorate over Tuva established in 1914, which brought Russian settlers to the upper Yenisei and preceded the Tuvan People's Republic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuva">Tuva</a>, called Uryankhay or Tannu Uriankhai, had been under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> as part of its Mongolian frontier. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a> collapse in 1911, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bogd-khanate-of-mongolia">Bogd Khanate of Mongolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> all claimed it. Some Tuvan nobles appealed to Russia, and in 1914 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The territory was administered by a Russian commissioner under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irkutsk">Irkutsk</a> governor-general, alongside traditional Tuvan princes and Buddhist monasteries. Russian peasant settlement increased, and in 1914 the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyzyl">Belotsarsk</a> (later Kyzyl) was founded as the administrative centre on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-river">Yenisei</a>. The Tuvan herding economy was little changed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1917 Russian administration collapsed, and Red and White forces, Chinese troops and Mongolian forces fought over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuva">Tuva</a>. With Soviet backing, Tuvan leaders proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tuvan-peoples-republic">Tuvan People's Republic</a> in August 1921.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/uryankhay-krai.jpg" alt="Uryankhay Krai" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Unknown , vector image created by MS05L · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Uriankhai_(1918-1921).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1914 CE – 1921 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Belotsarsk (Kyzyl)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism, Tuvan shamanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tuvan, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Uriankhai Territory, Tannu Uriankhai</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=uryankhay-krai" aria-label="Uryankhay Krai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6813 389 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Uryankhay Krai lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7133 645L6487 457M7133 645L7134 645"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6487 457h0M7134 645h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7133 645h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7133 645h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=uryankhay-krai">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
